  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "a9QalU0///U4XzSjRQHR620gf+0=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:06.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "A dead letter is a sync operation that failed and **cannot be retried** \u2014 a bad signature, a hash mismatch, an unrecoverable transport error. It is the terminal category, distinct from the transient failures that the channel retries with exponential backoff.\n\nWhen a channel routes an operation to its dead-letter mailbox the sync manager does four things: logs it, quarantines the document, persists a record to dead-letter storage, and emits `DEAD_LETTER_ADDED` (20004).\n\n```typescript\ntype DeadLetterAddedEvent = {\n  id: string;\n  jobId: string;\n  remoteName: string;\n  documentId: string;\n  errorSource: ChannelErrorSource; // none | channel | inbox | outbox\n};\n```\n\nThe design decision worth internalizing is the **granularity of the quarantine: per document, not per drive.** Once a document is quarantined, its inbound operations are dropped and its outbound operations are filtered out of the outbox \u2014 but every other document on the same remote keeps syncing normally. A single corrupt or unverifiable document therefore degrades to a hole in the replica rather than a stalled drive. Without this, one bad operation would head-of-line block an entire collection.\n\nThe flip side is that quarantine is silent unless you watch for it. Nothing retries automatically and nothing surfaces in the drive's own status, so `DEAD_LETTER_ADDED` is the event to subscribe to for anything that needs manual intervention. The `errorSource` tag tells you which stage produced it (`inbox`, `outbox`, or the `channel` itself), which is usually enough to separate \"the remote sent us something bad\" from \"we produced something the remote rejected\".\n\nRetention is bounded: each remote keeps at most `maxDeadLettersPerRemote` dead letters, default `100`, configurable via `SyncBuilder.withMaxDeadLettersPerRemote(limit)`. Past that the oldest are evicted \u2014 so dead-letter storage is a recent-failures buffer for diagnostics, not a durable audit log. If you need the full record, capture the events as they fire.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/01-Reactor/04-Synchronization.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:35:06.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:06.000Z"
    }
  },
